The == operator is used for primitive data types like int, float, and boolean. It compares the value of two objects directly without checking their contents or type. For example:

```java
int num1 = 5;
int num2 = 7;

System.out.println(num1 == num2); // Output: true

float num3 = 5.0f;
float num4 = 7.0f;

System.out.println(num3 == num4); // Output: false
```

On the other hand, the equals() method checks if two objects have the same content (i.e., they contain the same values). If you want to check if two strings are equal, use the equals() method:

```java
String str1 = "Hello";
String str2 = "Hello";

if (str1.equals(str2)) {
    System.out.println("Strings are equal");
} else {
    System.out.println("Strings are not equal");
}
```

In summary, == is good for comparing primitive data types like int, float, and boolean. But when you need to compare strings, use the equals() method. The main difference between the two operators is that == compares the value of two objects, while equals() checks if two objects have the same content.

